<binaryimage arch="x86:LE:32:default:gcc">
<!--
    kuna calleeretpurge: an i386 SysV struct-returning callee pops its own
    hidden result pointer with `ret $4`, and its caller is compiled around it.

    Source (gcc -m32 -O2 -fomit-frame-pointer):

        struct pt { int x, y, z; };
        struct pt mk(int a, int b) { struct pt p; p.x = a; p.y = b; p.z = a + b; return p; }
        int use(int k) {
          struct pt p = mk(k, 7);
          int local[4];
          local[0] = p.x; local[1] = p.y; local[2] = p.z; local[3] = k;
          sink(local);
          struct pt q = mk(local[1], k);
          return local[0] * local[1] + local[2] - local[3] + q.z;
        }

        8049190 mk:   mov eax,[esp+4] ... ret 4          ; pops the result pointer
        80491c0 use:  push esi; push ebx; sub esp,0x34
        80491c9       lea eax,[esp+8]                    ; &p
        80491cd       push 7; push esi; push eax
        80491d1       call mk                            ; esp afterwards = before - 8
        80491d6       mov eax,[esp+0x18]                 ; offsets assume the callee popped 4

    x86gcc.cspec's __cdecl states extrapop 4 for every callee, so the caller's
    stack pointer after each `call mk` is modelled four bytes too low: the
    first argument reads as `(int)&v3 + 4` and the frame slots after the call
    shift by one word.  The callee's `ret 4` is the machine's answer.

    Stage: P4 call prototypes (ActionDefaultParams), option calleeretpurge
    off|on.  Pass 1 (off) pins the bug, pass 2 (on, the default) the fix.
-->
